# TII Cross-Track Ion Drift Processor: Changes
# Copyright (C) 2022  Johnathan K Burchill

0302:
	Fixed attributes for latitude and longituge, which were swapped.
	Restored data from occasional gaps.
	Fix for CDF file zipping not to include file path.
0303:
	New calibration results incorporated from Summer 2021 testing.
	Fix for CDF file attribute.		
0402:
	Fixed Swarm B along-track scale factors at -62 V inner dome bias (used only with --do-not-use-eofr-for-along-track-drift).
0501:
	Reads 0501 calibration files from <calDir>/0501/Swarm_X/YYYY/TiiClbr0501_Swarm_X_YYYY_MM_DD.cdf.
	B is the 50 Hz VFM field averaged to 16 Hz (B-VFM) instead of the 1 Hz field upsampled. The processor
	rotates it to NEC (VFM to CRF with q_STR_VFM, CRF to NEC with q_NEC_CRF) and then to the satellite-track
	frame, defined as before by x parallel to the NEC satellite velocity. B, E and IGRF use the same frame as
	0401, with the velocity now in geocentric NEC (see Sat_Vel_N below). On 2015-10-18 (Swarm A) B differs
	from 0401 by 75, 1 and 36 nT RMS in x, y and z (140, 45 and 73 nT at most), mostly from that correction,
	and E by at most 0.05 mV/m RMS per component (2.2 mV/m at most).
	New variables: EhN EhE EhC, EvN EvE EvC, BN BE BC, VicrN VicrE VicrC, and IGRF from the cal file in
	satellite-track (IGRFx IGRFy IGRFz) and NEC (IGRFN IGRFE IGRFC) coordinates.
	Quality_flags bit 4: attitude OK, | |q_NEC_CRF| - 1 | <= 0.001. When 0, B, E, IGRF and the NEC variables are unreliable.
	Quality_flags bit 5: B OK, B-VFM-Flag is 0 and B-Avg-Flag is less than 2. When 0, B and E are unreliable.
	Users testing Quality_flags for equality (for example == 4) must now mask bits 0 to 3.
	Satellite velocity and corotation in the TII sensor frame are the cal-file Det_H__vX and "Det H CorVx", as
	in 0401. They need calibration files made after the EfiCal fix to its NEC axes, whose north pointed 50"
	(late 2015) from the Earth's rotation axis (cal_source/EfiCal_NEC_pole_bug_report.md). In earlier files
	Det_H__vY is off by 1 to 3 m/s at low and middle latitudes and by up to 38 m/s near the geographic poles
	(Swarm A, 2015-10-18), and Viy carries the same error, as in all earlier TCT versions. As a check, the
	processing log compares them with Sat_Vel_N and the corotation velocity rotated with q_NEC_CRF; fixed cal
	files should agree to about 0.1 m/s RMS.
	Sat_Vel_N in the calibration files has a WGS84 geodetic third component, not geocentric. It is converted
	to geocentric NEC before use (up to 24 m/s in VsatC). This corrects the satellite-track frame, whose x axis
	was up to 0.18 deg from the satellite velocity in earlier versions.
	The TII time shift from time tag to measurement time (1/32 - 0.0875 s) is applied as each calibration
	file is loaded, less any shift the file's global attribute TII_Time_Shift_Applied (s) says EfiCal has
	already applied (patch sent upstream). With EfiCal applying it, B-VFM, q_NEC_CRF, position, velocity and
	QD coordinates are evaluated at the measurement time instead of 56.25 ms after it. LP potentials and
	densities are now interpolated at the shifted times. For files without the attribute the output is unchanged.
	Swarm quaternion convention, checked against the cal file: with the scalar-last Hamilton matrix R(q),
	v_CRF = R(q_NEC_CRF) v_NEC and v_CRF = R(q_STR_VFM)^T v_VFM.
	Cal-file issue passed upstream: the 4 bad quaternions on 2015-10-18 come from interpolating across
	q -> -q sign flips in the 50 Hz attitude, which also corrupts IGRF-VFM at those records.
	Geoelectric potential integration skips NaN electric field samples (from NaN B).
	Fixed Calibration_flags, which could carry undefined bits from uninitialized memory.
	Fixed LP interpolation to TII times: it took the next LP sample instead of interpolating, and read past
	the end of the LP arrays for TII times after the last LP sample. U_SC and the ion density used for
	flagging are now linearly interpolated, and are NaN outside the span of the LP data.
	Global attributes: Instrument_type keeps all three entries (Electric Fields (space) was overwritten);
	Generation_date is ISO 8601 UTC; TEXT and Acknowledgement describe the magnetic field, IGRF and frames.
	As in 0401, the ion drifts and the corotation signal are in TII sensor axes, which differ from the
	satellite-track frame by the attitude control deadband (about +-4 deg in yaw).
